WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.710
(smooth music)

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:05.820
- What's up, everyone?

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.580
Michael here to talk about
the most recent episode

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.700
of "Rick and Morty,"
"Rick: A Mort Well Lived."

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.110
This one has it all.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.860
Rick starts a new religion
that's absolutely not a religion,

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.350
Morty's consciousness
gets shattered into pieces

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.170
inside a video game, and
Summer does a "Die Hard."

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.490
But something else stood out to us,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.410
and please just go with me on this one.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.780
The episode aired on 9/11,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.760
and we don't think this is a coincidence.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.890
It seems like the episode explores the way

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.660
in which religious narratives

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.880
and mythologies both shape human culture

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.300
and seem to fuel some
pretty serious violence.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.010
Now, I know this probably
sounds a little heavy

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.260
for an episode that
features Peter Dinklage

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.630
playing an alien obsessed with "Die Hard."

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:46.950
- It's not every day one meets

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.810
a fellow "Die Hard" enthusiast.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.910
- [Michael] But we'll explain
in this Wisecrack Quick Take

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.760
on "'Rick and Morty' Season 6, Episode 2:

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.160
Did 'Rick and Morty' Do a 9/11 Episode?"

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.420
First, a quick summary.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.360
Rick, Morty, and Summer
are at a space arcade

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.640
that's been taken over
by alien terrorists.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.490
Morty and Rick are both
inside the video game "Roy,"

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.830
which they previously played in season 2-

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.440
- Holy (beep)! This guy's
taking Roy off the grid!

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.630
- [Michael] and in which
Morty's consciousness is stuck,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.230
spread amongst all the non-Roy
characters in the game.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.440
Rick, as Roy, has to
convince all these characters

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.960
that they are in fact his grandson

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.180
in order to help Morty escape.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.060
All the while, Summer is
holding off the terrorists

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.730
after Rick told her to-
- Just do a "Die Hard."

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.590
- Eventually, Rick and Morty make it out.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.300
They help Summer to beat the terrorists.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.830
It's all happily ever after,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.600
except for the fact that part of, um,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.640
Morty's consciousness is now stuck

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.680
in the video game "Roy"
that's being wheeled

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.810
into a warehouse, "Indiana Jones"-style,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.090
potentially lost forever, but...

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:45.923
Oh, boy.

00:01:45.923 --> 00:01:46.800
But back to "Roy,"

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.410
the game in which Rick has
to convince all the Mortys

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:53.040
to accept the reality
that they are grandsons.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.890
It quickly turns into a
sort of religious movement.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.570
- He's one of those cult people.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.100
They worship some psycho named Roy

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.630
that says he's everybody's grandpa.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.400
- [Michael] And despite
Rick's best efforts-

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.440
- You guys keep thinking
I'm a religious leader.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.640
This isn't a religion.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.720
This is actual truth.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.190
I'm a scientist in real life.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:09.540
- [Michael] This all
seems to be the show's way

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:11.820
of pointing out the often arbitrary roots

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.380
of various religious movements-

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.600
- I feel like every time I
explain this, I lose followers,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.160
but this isn't a religion.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.230
- [Michael] and the eagerness
with which people join them

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.520
and build a culture around them.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.280
But this non-religion
religion, grandsonism,

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.400
quickly establishes a type
of in-group, out-group logic

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.340
in which those acknowledging
that they are 14-year-old boys

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.580
who love masturbation are in
and those who remain attached

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.410
to their video game selves are out.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.030
This is of course a thing that's happened

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.110
throughout human history,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.930
often with less-than-kosher consequences.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:40.763
- Get down!

00:02:40.763 --> 00:02:42.990
You're all under arrest for
being religious the wrong way!

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.490
- [Michael] What starts as a little

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.920
old-fashioned social
division based on religion

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.970
quickly escalates to a full-on
holy war, which is set off

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.030
when Rick struggles to tell
Morty that he loves him.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.190
- Rick, I'll get every single part of me

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:57.780
to get on every ship and go with you.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.613
- Good.

00:02:58.613 --> 00:03:00.183
- But you have to tell us you love us.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.050
- I mean, at this point,
even if he said it,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.550
what-what-what-what's with the pause?

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.050
- Screw you! Wait for it.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.760
- Holy war!
- Holier war!

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.900
- Holiest war ever!

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.090
- I guess the message is
tell people you love them

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.290
or you might cause a war.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.620
The use of the term holy war
doesn't seem to be accidental,

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.840
as the show is clearly pointing out

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.930
how quickly religious
beliefs can become excuses

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.550
for large-scale violence.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.360
And again, um, this episode aired on 9/11.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.240
And the show isn't original
in making the argument

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.120
that religion has an exceptional ability

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.820
to incite violence and war, and fittingly,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.530
some of the most potent
religious narratives

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.710
that justify violence
are, like grandsonism,

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.440
not explicitly religious per se.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.180
While some would frame the war on terror

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.780
as liberal democracy versus
Islamic fundamentalism,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.910
others might see the opposite,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.720
i.e., a pseudo-theological
American nationalism

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.480
versus a region of the
world devastated by years

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.060
of foreign intervention
and military occupation.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.980
And that's not even to
mention oil production

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.260
or Dick Cheney's business friends.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.880
However, the conventional way

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.950
that the war on terror is framed

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.450
serves an important purpose.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.917
According to theologian
William T. Cavanaugh,

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:14.730
"the myth of religious
violence promotes a dichotomy

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.650
between us in the secular West,

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.250
who are rational and
peacemaking, and them, the hordes

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.530
of violent religious
fanatics in the Muslim world.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.420
Their violence is religious

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.490
and therefore irrational and divisive.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.380
Our violence, on the other hand,

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.137
is rational, peacemaking, and necessary.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.510
Regrettably, we find ourselves forced

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.790
to bomb them into the higher rationality."

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:36.990
So for Cavanaugh,

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.330
the idea that religion is uniquely violent

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.000
is often more of an ideological function

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.280
dependent on specific definitions

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.470
of what's religion and what's not

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:49.560
used to justify more rational forms

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.090
of non-religious violence

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.640
often carried out in
the name of the state.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.700
It seems that the episode
might be leaning into the idea

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.160
that the seemingly decades-long holy war

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.120
between grandson factions is
a reflection of the absurdity

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.760
of lots of actually
existing human conflict

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.700
in which people are willing
to kill or be killed

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.240
over disagreements on religious
and mythical narratives.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.230
- It's parenting like this

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.060
that pushed me into religious radicalism.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.820
- So is "Rick and Morty"
using the holy wars

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.370
of grandsonism to argue
that religion is both,

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.400
A, often a totally made-up thing that, B,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.510
is unique in its ability to
create irrational violence?

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.580
Maybe, but according to Cavanaugh,

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.460
it's a mistake to attempt
to draw a distinction

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.627
between secular and religious violence.

00:05:34.627 --> 00:05:37.320
"We must conclude that
there is no coherent way

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.920
to isolate religious ideologies
with a peculiar tendency

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.280
towards violence from their
tamer secular counterparts.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.830
So-called secular
ideologies and institutions

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:50.760
like nationalism and liberalism
can be just as absolutist,

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.670
divisive, and irrational
as so-called religion.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.590
People kill for all sorts of things.

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.840
The point is not simply
that secular violence

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.660
should be given equal attention
to religious violence.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.120
The point is that the
distinction between secular

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.240
and religious violence
is unhelpful, misleading,

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.420
and mystifying and should
be avoided together."

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.660
So basically, it's not that
religion is uniquely violent

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.360
in history, especially given
that, according to Cavanaugh,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.300
the secular-religious dichotomy
is a modern invention.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.510
Rather, it's that
absolutism and irrationality

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.970
are at the heart of
all political violence.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.280
So unlike the Sam Harrises and Bill Mahers

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.620
of the world who argue
that Western violence

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.960
towards the Islamic world is justifiable

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.470
due to the superior rationality
of liberal democracy,

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.930
for Cavanaugh, there
isn't really a distinction

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:40.290
between so-called religious
and secular violence.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.420
Okay, so if that's the case,

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.450
then why do we still
often associate religion

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.340
with irrational violence?

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:50.070
Well, for Cavanaugh, "the
myth of religious violence

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:53.850
establishes a reassuring
dichotomy between their violence,

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.370
which is absolutist,
divisive, and irrational,

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.030
and our violence, which is
modest, unitive, and rational."

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.670
Making the violence of the
other uniquely religious

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.950
allows those on the other
side to feel confident

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:08.430
that they're violent
but, like, in a good way.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.080
- There isn't even a
God in the real world,

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.370
you fragmented putz.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:14.130
- This also involves
the type of absolutism

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.250
that both religious and
secular violence have,

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:21.150
because both sides believe
they have absolute access

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.520
to some type of goodness or truth.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.950
And then they have a
mission to expand the reach

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:27.780
of that goodness or truth.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.370
And speaking of goodness and truth,

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.290
have you checked out "Wisecrack Live" yet?

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:32.730
It's our new stream.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.310
It takes place every
Thursday at 11:00 am Pacific

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.620
right here on our YouTube page.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:38.453
It's fun.

00:07:38.453 --> 00:07:41.160
We watch videos, talk about
what's going on in the world.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.320
We have a really fun chat,
and I, I really mean that.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.730
It's, like, the best chat on YouTube.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.170
So come check it out sometime.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:47.340
We would love to have you there.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.380
Okay, so these themes are also present

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.330
in Summer's storyline, as "Die Hard"

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.030
has become a foundational
myth across the galaxy.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.550
- Every sentient civilization
across the galaxy

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.590
eventually develops the same myth.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.720
In Centauri, it's known as "Tower Man."

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.330
The Orionese have their "Thornberg Cycle."

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:05.940
And in Andromeda, it's called
"foolish to have imagined

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.140
you'd be able to kill."

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:09.750
- [Michael] And the potency
of this myth has clearly led

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.820
to lots of very stupid violence.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.770
- Instead of handing me an unloaded gun,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.950
she'll hand me a-
- "Die Hard"!

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:17.430
"Die Hard"! "Die Hard"!

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.040
- [Michael] Summer only
reads this religious text

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:23.010
when it helps her achieve
her goal of killing aliens.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:24.390
- You left your book in the bathroom.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:25.590
I read it while I took a (beep).

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.120
I mean, mostly the ending.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.340
- [Michael] The quasi-religious
mythology of "Die Hard"

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.730
is reduced into a mere
conduit for more violence.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.130
- Walkie-talkie "Die Hard," (beep).

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.680
- So what is the show saying here?

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.810
On the one hand, they
might be pointing out

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:42.420
the seeming absurdity
of religion in general

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:44.130
and more specifically its tendency

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:46.770
to engender violence
amongst its adherents.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.890
On the other, maybe they're
taking a more broad approach

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.180
and showing how, in the wake

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.190
of events like the 9/11 attacks,

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:56.160
politics and religion
can be just two sides

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.980
of the same irrationally violent coin.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.140
- Better to die in "Roy"
than to serve in hell!

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.660
- [Michael] As Kavanaugh
concludes, "this opposition

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.750
of irrational and irrational,
secular and religious,

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.660
Western and Muslim is not
simply descriptive but helps

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:13.440
to create the opposition that
it purports to describe."

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.330
Well said, William. Well said.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:16.590
But what do you guys think?

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:18.330
Did Justin Roiland and co.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:21.480
make a cynical holiday episode for 9/11,

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:22.650
or has growing up during the war

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.420
on terror just broken my brain?

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:26.160
Please let us know in the comments.

